nything AgentKit does, a third party can do too."],[1577,"That is the same shape as accepting a config object, except the surface is every row rather than the keys you thought to expose."],[783,"The @agentkit/core package ships a replay binary — no custom script required:"],[702,"The @agentkit/workspace package provides sandboxed file and shell tools plus createCodingAgentPreset() for Claude Code–style agents. The Code CLI example (examples/code-cli) is a thin terminal client over agent.session() — the studio example (examples/studio) uses the same workspace as a scratch sandbox in a chat UI."],[2006,"the agent microkernel on that kernel: session log, tool pipeline, prompt assembly, model seam, replaceable loop."],[667,"The agentkit-replay binary ships from @agentkit/core. See Harness."],[1827,"The answer must match a schema"],[1066,"The API reference is generated from TSDoc comments on @agentkit/core during the docs build."],[2142,"the append-only event log and its projection"],[1483,"The append-only session log with a seq on every event"],[1097,"the artifact's contents, read with the token and tenant: as is \"text\" (default), \"json\", \"blob\" or \"arrayBuffer\""],[1663,"The bindings are therefore built inside the vm, around a single host callback captured in a closure, with arguments crossing as JSON. Inside a program, globalThis holds only tools and log; import is blocked, process is undefined, and an infinite loop is cut off by the vm timeout."],[1025,"The context a rule sees: messages, lastUser (context a runtime injects mid-turn is not counted as the user), toolResults (this turn), results (the same, decoded: { name, value } with JSON parsed and compacted results unwrapped), tools (names offered), system, and match (the regex result)."],[1193,"the current plan with step statuses, or undefined"],[1544,"The daemon. A long-lived process that owns the runtime, mounts handleSessionRoutes behind your auth, and maps users to sessions through its scope callback."],[2208,"The difference between the two mechanisms matters. A tools/pre-execute listener is policy — reorderable, overridable, and the right home for \"ask the user\". A guard is an invariant — it only ever adds refusals, and a derived registry inherits it, so moving into a realm cannot escape it."],[363,"The engine stays TypeScript. The wire protocol is the standard; language clients stay thin."],[2255,"The exec handle"],[2110,"the execution world is one row; the tests swap it for an in-memory one and every tool follows"],[1573,"The first is static: the resolved row list after patches, before anything runs. The second is live: the fiber tree, including any plugin still blocked on a dependency. Between them, \"why is my plugin not doing anything\" is usually a one-line answer."],[2109,"the fs + subprocess seams"],[1645,"The graph is validated in register(), not at run time — a cycle or a dangling needs is a mistake in the definition, and it should fail while its author is looking at it."],[1688,"the harness does"],[1907,"The hook itself is one line of config in the bundle: a PreToolUse shell command that reads the call on stdin and exits non-zero to refuse it."],[1017,"The ids live in NEMOTRON_MODELS. Any other OpenAI-compatible host that serves Nemotron (OpenRouter, vLLM, your gateway) works through registerProvider as shown below. createNvidiaProvider({ apiKey?, baseURL? }) builds the NVIDIA provider directly."],[1203,"the ids the hub knows"],[2226,"The invariant"],[383,"The iterator ends on the terminal done/error event; on failure stream.result rejects. Breaking out of the loop (or stream.cancel()) unsubscribes — on a session.stream(), cancel() also cancels the underlying run. For a ready-made terminal view, pass the stream to streamToConsole(...)."],[1594,"The kernel and the runtime give you a turn. What makes it a harness is the capability layer on top: the things an agent can actually do, each behind a seam so you can swap the implementation without touching the tools."],[2114,"The last four ship from @agentkit/core — they are not example code, they are the batteries, mounted the same way yours would be."],[760,"The likely tools: the best keyword matches between the task and each tool's name,\nargument names and description (6 by default), plus anything in alwaysLoad and any tool the\nrun or session allows by name (tools.allow)."],[1625,"The list is not projected into model history. It reaches the model as a prompt section rendering current state, so a hundred edits cost one rendering, while the log still holds every revision for a UI to replay."],[2070,"The live smoke test"],[1887,"The log is the memory"],[2107,"the log survives a restart, so fork and resume are real"],[941,"The model registry resolves the active provider and model on every session.send() and agent.run(). Call agent.models.setDefault(modelId) (or the wire/client equivalent) and the next turn uses the new model — no manual provider re-instantiation."],[1531,"The model writes a program against the tool bindings"],[1649,"The model writes a program instead of a call — and where that program runs is a seam"],[1851,"The model writes its own delegates, in parallel"],[1919,"The old extension surface was a config schema, and a config schema can only express features its author already imagined. Denying a tool call, wrapping a dispatch with a deadline, rewriting what the model sees, or pointing the filesystem at a remote sandbox each meant patching the runtime."],[1798,"The ordering is the security property. A subprocess/spawn listener can vet or rewrite a command, but the sandbox rewrite happens last, so no plugin can rewrite its way out of it — and swapping the spawner provider does not drop the confinement."],[1146,"The package ships a dependency-free ES module build, so no bundler is needed:"],[767,"The plugin kernel has the same behaviour through deferredTools; see\nContext engineering."],[799,"The reasoning loop also emits log at info level for tool starts (